NAME
       neo - text editor

SYNOPSIS
       neo [file]

DESCRIPTION
       A full-screen editor in the style of nano: the text fills the
       screen, the cursor sits where you type, and the keys are shown
       at the bottom.

KEYS
       Ctrl-S   save
       Ctrl-Q   exit, twice if there are unsaved changes
       Esc      exit as well
       Ctrl-F   search
       Ctrl-G   go to a line number
       Ctrl-X   cut the current line
       Ctrl-C   copy the current line
       Ctrl-V   paste
       Ctrl-D   duplicate the current line
       Ctrl-B   jump to the start of the file
       Ctrl-E   jump to the end of the file
       Ctrl-N   line numbers on or off
       Ctrl-L   pick the language for colouring
       Ctrl-T   file tree
       Ctrl-P   settings
       Ctrl-R   offer completions for the word being typed
       arrows, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move around

SETTINGS
       Ctrl-P opens the settings. Left and right change a value, Enter
       acts on a line that says <Enter>.

         Auto-pairs       type ( and get ), and the same for quotes
         Line numbers     a number column down the left edge
         Syntax           which language the colouring follows
         Tab width        how wide a tab is drawn, 1 to 16
         Quit confirm     ask before leaving an unsaved file
         Expand tabs      insert spaces instead of a tab character
         Auto indent      a new line starts where the last one did
         Trim on save     drop trailing whitespace when saving
         Highlight line   shade the line the cursor is on
         Show tabs        draw tabs as > and dots, to see them
         Backup on save   copy the old file to <name>.bak first
         Scroll margin    lines kept above and below the cursor, 0 to 20
         Autocomplete     offer words as you type, see COMPLETION
         Edit syntax rules  the colouring editor, see below
         Save settings    write them to ~/.neorc

       ~/.neorc is read at startup, so the settings survive a reboot on
       an installed system.

FILES
       ~/.neorc

SEE ALSO
       cfm(1), less(1)

COMPLETION
       After two characters of a word, a list appears under the cursor
       with what that word could become. Up and down move through it,
       Enter or Tab takes the one that is highlighted, Esc dismisses
       it. Ctrl-R asks for the list without waiting, and re-reads the
       headers first.

       The list is not every word in the file. It is, in order:

         the words of the current language, from its .syn file, each
         labelled with the group it came from

         the functions and macros of every header the file includes -
         a file with #include <string.h> offers strlen and strcmp,
         marked 'lib'

         words already in this file, marked 'file', or 'here' when
         they come from the line being edited

       Headers are looked for in /usr/include, then /mnt/usr/include,
       then the current directory. Autocomplete in the settings turns
       the whole thing off.

SYNTAX COLOURING
       Every language the editor knows is a file in /etc/neo, read at
       startup. A .syn file put there is a language neo understands
       from the next time it runs; nothing is compiled in. Ctrl-L
       lists them, with the file endings each one claims.

       One setting per line, name = value; blank lines and lines
       beginning # are ignored.

         name       what to call it in the status bar
         ext        the file endings it claims, separated by spaces
         comment    what starts a comment running to end of line
         block      what opens and closes a block comment, two words
         strings    single if ' quotes as well as ", double if only "
         backtick   yes if backticks quote a string
         mode       html for tag-and-attribute colouring instead

       Colours are six hex digits, as on a web page:

         color comment = 6a737d
         color string  = 98c379
         color number  = d19a66
         color escape  = 61afef
         color default = c8ccd4

       Words are put in named groups, and the group carries its own
       colour. The name is yours to choose - it is what the completion
       list shows next to the word:

         words keyword c678dd = begin end if then else while do
         words type e5c07b = integer real char boolean string
         words builtin 56b6c4 = writeln readln length

       A group may be repeated over several lines; the words add up.
       'chars' instead of 'words' matches anywhere rather than only on
       whole words, which is how to colour operators:

         chars operator 61afef = + - * / := <> <= >=

       For example, a complete Pascal file:

         name = Pascal
         ext = pas pp inc
         comment = //
         block = (* *)
         strings = single

         color comment = 6a737d
         color string  = 98c379

         words keyword c678dd = begin end if then else while do
         words type e5c07b = integer real char boolean string

       /etc/neo/c.syn and the others that ship are worked examples.

THE SYNTAX EDITOR
       Writing the file by hand is optional. Edit syntax rules in the
       settings opens a form for the same thing, and it does not need
       a file open: on a bare neo it offers the languages already there
       to edit, or a new one to start.

       Moving highlights a line and the text below says what it does.
       Enter changes the highlighted setting. On a colour it opens a
       dialog with a slider per channel and a sample of the result, or
       h to type the value as hex. On a group, Enter edits its words,
       c its colour, r its name and d removes it; Ctrl-G or the last
       line adds one.

       Ctrl-S asks for a file name and writes the rules, then applies
       them to the open file without leaving the editor. When a Cervus
       system is mounted at /mnt the file goes to /mnt/etc/neo so it
       survives a reboot; otherwise it goes to /etc/neo, which a live
       session forgets.
